{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 20, :comments ["<b>And Noah began, a man of the soil, and planted a vineyard.</b> וַיָּחֶל is a term of חֻלִּין (profanity), for profanity is the opposite of holiness, and wherever you find a boundary against immorality, there you find holiness. But wine habituates a person to immorality, as it is written (Hosea 4:11), ״זְנוּת וְיַיִן וְתִירוֹשׁ יִקַּח לֵב״, and everyone who sees a sotah in her corruption should abstain from wine. Therefore the verse uses the term ״וַיָּחֶל״ with regard to him, for through this planting he became profane and was uncovered within his tent. And thus it will be explained further on, with the help of HaShem, in the verse ״וַיָּחֶל הָעָם לִזְנוֹת״ (Numbers 25:1), and see also in parashat Naso regarding the matter of the nazirite and the sotah. And it called him ״אִישׁ הָאֲדָמָה״ (a man of the soil), for this deed was from the side of matter, which the soil brings forth, but the intellect that is in man — a divine portion from above — does not consent to this planting, for wine confuses a man's intellect." "Another matter: <b>it called him a man of the soil,</b> for he followed in the ways of the first man, Adam, who sinned through the grapevine — according to the one who says the Tree of Knowledge was a vine. Another matter: that wine causes his spirit to depart and return to his soil before its time, as it is written (Proverbs 14:12), ״יֵשׁ דֶּרֶךְ יָשָׁר לִפְנֵי אִישׁ וְאַחֲרִיתָהּ דַּרְכֵי מָוֶת״. ״יֵשׁ דֶּרֶךְ יָשָׁר״ — the last letters spell ״שֵׁכָר״ (strong drink), for every drunkard walks upright and all forbidden relations seem to him as level ground — thus they expounded in the Gemara (Yoma 75a) — and not literally forbidden relations alone, but every sin and every transgression: everything seems straight in his eyes, and he does not know that it is against his own life, for wine consumes from soul to flesh. Therefore it says ״אַחֲרִיתָהּ״ — that is, the final letters of the words from ״יֵשׁ דֶּרֶךְ יָשָׁר״ are ״דַּרְכֵי מָוֶת״, for intoxicating drink is a path of death. And a hint to Noah: when you write ״יַיִן״ — yod, yod, nun — the final letters yield the numerical value of ״נֹחַ״, hinting that he was the first to engage in this planting." "And the Sages said (Genesis Rabbah 36:4), ״וַיִּתְגַּל בְּתוֹךְ אָהֳלֹה״ — that he caused his sons exile, through the sin of drinking from wine goblets. And perhaps this is the intent of the midrash brought in the Yalkut (Genesis 9:20), that the Satan encountered Noah when he planted the vineyard, and slaughtered before him first a lamb, then a lion, and then a pig, etc. There is in the hidden things a hint to the three known exiles: the exile of Egypt, when they were enslaved to the lamb; and the exile of Babylon, through Nebuchadnezzar, who was called a lion, as it is written (Jeremiah 4:7), ״עָלָה אַרְיֵה מִסֻּבְּכוֹ״; and a third exile through the nation of whom it is said, ״יְכַרְסְמֶנָּה חֲזִיר מִיָּעַר״ (Psalms 80:14). And the word ״עֲנָבִים״ — its partner is סַמָּאֵל through the second letters in the aleph-bet, a fitting hint that the Satan encountered him through the planting of the grapes."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 9, :notes {1 "The gematria of the final letters of יין: the word has two letters — final nun of the first יין (50) plus final nun of the second (50) = 100, which equals נח (50+8=58) only if counted differently; translated the wordplay as he states it.", 2 "The wordplay 'שותפו של סמאל' through 'second letters' (תמורת אתבש or similar atbash-style letter substitution) is translated as stated; the mechanical substitution system is not further glossed."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:27:58.879099538Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}